          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em ring
            (also:
            = succeed ")" do
              %em number ring
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            equipped with two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+operation"} binary operations
            called
            %em addition
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em multiplication
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            and the operation of multiplication a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            structure which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributivity+law"} distributes
            over addition.
          %p
            All the familiar number systems such as the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer+numbers"} integer numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex"} complex
            numbers are rings under the standard operations of addition and multiplication. Except for the first in this list they are indeed
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fields"} fields
          %p
            Other basic examples of rings are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+groups"} cyclic groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_n
            under their mod-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            operations inherited from the integers (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers+modulo+n"}> integers modulo n
            ); the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+rings"} polynomial rings
          %p
            More abstractly, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            (with their
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+abelian+groups"} tensor product of abelian groups
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+objects"} monoid objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
            and more generally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+infinity-categories"} stable infinity-categories
            constitute generalizations of the notion of
            %em ring
            that are of interest. Notably when abelian groups are generalized to their analogs in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-infinity+rings"} E-infinity rings
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
          %p
            Rings form a category,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ring"} Ring
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CRing"} CRing
          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              (unital and not-necessarily commutative) is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              equipped with
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  an element
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in R
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear+map"} bilinear map
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+homomorphism"} group homomorphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo :
                        %mi R
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi R
                        %mo →
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\cdot : R \otimes R \to R
                %p
                  out of the
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+abelian+groups"} tensor product of abelian groups
            %p
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity+law"} associative
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+law"} unital
              with respect to 1.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The fact that the product is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear+map"} bilinear map
              is the
              = succeed ":" do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributivity+law"} distributivity law
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r, r_1, r_2 \in R
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi r
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi r
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    r \cdot (r_1 + r_2) = r \cdot r_1 + r \cdot r_2
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi r
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi r
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi r
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (r_1 + r_2) \cdot r = r_1 \cdot r + r_2 \cdot r
                    \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A (unital, non-commutative)
              %strong ring
              is (equivalently)
            %ul
              %li
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
                regarded as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                equipped with the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+abelian+groups"} tensor product of abelian groups
              %li
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} category enriched over
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
                with a single
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %li
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringoid"} ringoid
                with a single
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %p
              A
              %strong commutative
              (unital) ring is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative monoid
              object in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Ab
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Ab, \otimes)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              In usual ring theory people often talk about
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonunital+rings"} nonunital rings
              as well: multiplicative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semigroups"} semigroups
              with additive
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
              structure where the multiplication is distributive toward addition; these are semigroup objects in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ab
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
              = succeed "." do
                %strong commutative nonunital
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/red+herring+principle"} red herring principle
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              If one removes the assumption that the additive group is abelian but retains the remaining ring axioms, the result is still a ring. More generally, this holds for nonunital rings of which multiplicative semigroup is left/right
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+reductive+semigroup"} weakly reductive
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (G, +, 0)
              we could define multiplication to be the
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\cdot y := 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
              doesn’t distinguish between elements of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            It is possible to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalise
            the notion of ring in at least two different ways. Either one can replace the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} category of sets
            in the classical definition with another category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            – see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+object"} ring object
            – , or one can replace
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            in the fancy definition with another category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %h3#internalising_the_sets Internalising the sets
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            may be internalised in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %em ring objects
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is simply a ring. (This works whether your rings are unital or nonunital, commutative or noncommutative, etc.) However, not every notion of internal ring takes this form.
          %p
            The theory of rings is a combination of a monoid (or semigroup, if nonunital) and an abelian group structure. Thus, ring objects are algebras over a composed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            of a monoid monad and an abelian group monad, using a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+law"} distributive law
          %p
            A particular example of this is a ring in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %p
            Picking a ring object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            promotes it into a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed topos
          %p
            In cartesian categories one can also define the structure of an (abelian) group object as the lifting of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+presheaf"} representable presheaf
            to a presheaf into (abelian) groups. This kind of lifting of some algebraic structure in sets to algebraic structure in a cartesian category makes sense when some category of algebras creates the limits needed to define them in sets.
          %h3#internalising_the_abelian_groups Internalising the abelian groups
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+objects"} monoid objects
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            to be somewhat like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
            to think of monoid objects in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            as internal rings. For example, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is the category of abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+objects"} group objects
            in a cartesian monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            from above. Or, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab-enriched+category"} Ab-enriched category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
            that we may consider its monoid objects as internal rings. There are yet other examples, however: a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+spectrum"} ring spectrum
            is a monoid object in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            = succeed "-enriched." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
          %p
            Other examples are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+ring"} simplicial rings
            (as monoids in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+abelian+group"} simplicial abelian groups
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-ring"} dg-rings
            = succeed "-rings" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h3#_rings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+over+a+ring"} Rings over a ring
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            \-rings)
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is a commutative ring (or especially a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is a monoid object in
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a noncommutative ring, then a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                ring over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                \-ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            in
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bimod"} Bimod
            (that is, in
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo
                      %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} _A Mod _A
            = succeed "-ring" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            to the usual rings. In fact the unit map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to R
            is a morphism of rings, and the category of
            = succeed "-rings" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coslice+category"} coslice category
            or under-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi Ring
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A/Ring
            = succeed "-rings" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %em under
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "-rings" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %em over
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %em over
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %p
            Unlike for the
            = succeed "-algebras," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ×
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\times R\to R
            which is the morphism of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %em second
            factor, but only
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{op}
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k (r s) = r (k s)
            is not true, for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\in A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ,
                    %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r,s\in R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k (r s) = (k r) s
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (r s) k = r (s k)
            do hold.
          %p
            Both for a discussion for under-over and also for this difference between
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "-rings" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/12/a_quick_algebra_quiz.html"} quick algebra quiz
          %p
            A dual notion to an
            = succeed "-ring" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coring"} coring
          %p
            The structure of an
            = succeed "-ring" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes A^{op}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R,\mu,\eta)
            is determined by the structure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as a ring, together with the two natural homomorphisms of rings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo =
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s = \eta(-\otimes 1_A):A\to R
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t=\eta(1_A\otimes -):A^{op}\to R
            which have commuting images (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(a)t(a')=t(a')s(a)
            , for all
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,a'\in A
          %h3#higher_rings Higher rings
          %p
            By replacing in the sentence “a ring is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            in
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category
            of
            %em symmetric monoidal
            higher groupoids, one obtains higher notion of rings, such as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+groupoid"} ring groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+ring+groupoid"} symmetric ring groupoid
          %p
            Of particular interest is the maximal case of symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoids
            and, even more generally, that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monoid in an (∞,1)-category
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+spectra"} stable (∞,1)-category of spectra
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity-ring"} A-infinity-ring
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+ring+spectrum"} associative ring spectrum
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} commutative monoid in an (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-infinity+ring"} E-infinity ring
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring+spectrum"} commutative ring spectrum
          %h2#Examples Examples
